editorial:
## Brute force — O(n^2)

Try every pair. Correct, and at `n = 100000` it is 10^10 operations, so it times out. Write it anyway as the baseline: it defines what the fast version must agree with.

## The waste

For each `i` we re-scan the whole array looking for `target - a[i]`. That lookup is the repeated work. A hash map turns it into O(1).

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(n) space

One pass. For each element, ask whether its complement has already been seen.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    long long n, target;
    cin >> n >> target;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    unordered_map<long long, long long> seen;   // value -> index
    for (long long i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        auto it = seen.find(target - a[i]);
        if (it != seen.end()) {
            cout << it->second << ' ' << i << '\n';
            return 0;
        }
        // Insert AFTER the lookup. Inserting first would let an element
        // pair with itself whenever target == 2 * a[i].
        seen[a[i]] = i;
    }
    cout << "-1\n";
}
```

## Why the insert order matters

On input `2 6` / `3 3` the answer is `0 1`. If you insert `a[0] = 3` before looking up `6 - 3 = 3`, you find index 0 and print `0 0` — using one position twice. Inserting after the lookup guarantees `it->second < i`, which also gives the required ordering for free.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Brute force | O(n^2) | O(1) |
| Hash map | O(n) | O(n) |
| Sort + two pointers | O(n log n) | O(n) for original indices |

The sorting approach is worth knowing but is strictly worse here, and sorting destroys the positions you have to report.

