editorial:
## Three approaches

**Brute force, O(n^2).** Compare every pair. Times out at 10^5.

**Sort, then scan adjacent pairs — O(n log n), O(1) extra.** After sorting, duplicates are neighbours. Use this when memory is tight.

**Hash set — O(n) average, O(n) space.** Insert as you go; if an insert finds the value already present, you are done.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    unordered_set<int> seen;
    seen.reserve(n * 2);        // avoids ~log n rehashes

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        // insert returns {iterator, inserted?}. A false second means the
        // value was already there — one lookup instead of two.
        if (!seen.insert(x).second) {
            cout << "true\n";
            return 0;
        }
    }
    cout << "false\n";
}
```

The early return matters: on an array whose first two elements match, this reads two values rather than all 10^5.

## Edge cases

`n = 0` and `n = 1` must both print `false` — there is nothing to duplicate. A loop that assumes at least two elements gets these wrong.

## The sorting alternative

```cpp
// O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space beyond the input.
sort(a.begin(), a.end());
for (size_t i = 1; i < a.size(); ++i)
    if (a[i] == a[i - 1]) { cout << "true\n"; return 0; }
cout << "false\n";
```

Note `i = 1` and comparing backwards. Writing `i < a.size() - 1` would underflow on an empty array, as the undefined-behaviour lesson warns.

