## Counting — O(n) time, O(1) space

The alphabet is 26 letters, so the count table is fixed-size regardless of `n`. That is what makes the space O(1) rather than O(n).

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    string a, b;
    cin >> a >> b;

    // Different lengths can never be anagrams, and checking first avoids
    // scanning two large strings for nothing.
    if (a.size() != b.size()) {
        cout << "false\n";
        return 0;
    }

    array<int, 26> count{};              // {} zero-initialises; omitting it
                                         // leaves garbage — see the UB lesson
    for (char c : a) ++count[c - 'a'];
    for (char c : b) --count[c - 'a'];

    for (int c : count) {
        if (c != 0) {
            cout << "false\n";
            return 0;
        }
    }
    cout << "true\n";
}
```

One table, incremented for the first string and decremented for the second. If every entry returns to zero the multiset of characters matched exactly. Two separate tables and a comparison also works; one table is less code and makes the invariant clearer.

## The sorting alternative — O(n log n)

```cpp
sort(a.begin(), a.end());
sort(b.begin(), b.end());
cout << (a == b ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
```

Three lines and obviously correct. Prefer it when `n` is small or you are short on time; the counting version when the input is large.

## Generalising

For arbitrary Unicode, swap `array<int,26>` for `unordered_map<char,int>` — O(n) space, but no assumption about the alphabet. The length check stays the first thing you do.

