## The observation

To sell on day `i` for the most profit, you want to have bought at the **cheapest price among days 0..i-1**. So a single pass that tracks the minimum seen so far is enough — there is nothing to reconsider, which is what makes this greedy and not DP.

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) space

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    int lowest = INT_MAX;
    int best = 0;              // 0, because doing nothing is permitted

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int p;
        cin >> p;
        // Order matters: update the minimum first and `best` could use
        // today's price as both buy and sell, which the problem forbids.
        // Evaluating the sale against the PREVIOUS minimum keeps buy < sell.
        best = max(best, p - lowest);
        lowest = min(lowest, p);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

Note the ordering. `best` is computed against `lowest` *before* today's price is folded in, which is what enforces "sell strictly later than buy". Swapping the two lines yields `p - p = 0` as a candidate, which happens to be harmless here only because `best` starts at 0 — but it is wrong reasoning, and the same mistake breaks the variant where you must trade.

## Why `best` starts at 0, not INT_MIN

The problem permits doing nothing, so on a strictly decreasing array the answer is 0. This is the mirror image of the maximum-subarray trap, where initialising to 0 is *wrong* because an empty selection is not allowed there. The initial value encodes whether the empty choice is legal — read the statement for it rather than copying a habit.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Brute force (every pair) | O(n^2) | O(1) |
| One pass | O(n) | O(1) |

