## Kadane's algorithm

At each element there are exactly two choices: extend the subarray ending at the previous element, or start a new one here. Take whichever is larger.

The reason this is safe: if the running sum has gone negative, it can only hurt anything that follows, so discarding it is never wrong.

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) space

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;

    // 🛑 LLONG_MIN, not 0. A non-empty subarray is required, so on an
    // all-negative array the answer is negative. Initialising to 0 is the
    // single most common wrong answer on this problem.
    long long best = LLONG_MIN;
    long long cur = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        // Either start fresh at x, or extend the previous run.
        cur = max(x, cur + x);
        best = max(best, cur);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

## Dry run on the sample

| x | cur = max(x, cur+x) | best |
|---|---|---|
| -2 | -2 | -2 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 |
| -3 | -2 | 1 |
| 4 | 4 | 4 |
| -1 | 3 | 4 |
| 2 | 5 | 5 |
| 1 | **6** | **6** |
| -5 | 1 | 6 |
| 4 | 5 | 6 |

Notice `cur` drops to 1 at the `-5` and the answer is already banked in `best`. Tracking both is what lets the algorithm abandon a run without losing the result.

## As dynamic programming

Kadane is a rolled 1-D DP. The state is `dp[i]` = best sum of a subarray *ending at* `i`, with transition `dp[i] = max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])`. Since `dp[i]` depends only on `dp[i-1]`, the array collapses to one variable — exactly the rolling trick from the DP lesson.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Every subarray | O(n^2) | O(1) |
| Divide and conquer | O(n log n) | O(log n) |
| Kadane | O(n) | O(1) |

