editorial:
## Why not division

The tempting solution is "total product divided by `a[i]`". It fails on zeros: with one zero every other answer is 0 and the zero's own answer is the product of the rest — and division by zero is undefined. The no-division rule exists to force the real technique.

## The idea: prefix and suffix products

The product of everything except position `i` is

```
(product of a[0..i-1])  *  (product of a[i+1..n-1])
```

Both factors are computable in one pass each. This is the prefix-sum idea with multiplication in place of addition.

## Optimal — O(n) time, O(1) extra space

The output array doubles as the prefix store, so no additional array is needed.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    vector<long long> out(n, 1);

    // Pass 1, left to right: out[i] = product of everything BEFORE i.
    long long running = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        out[i] = running;
        running *= a[i];
    }

    // Pass 2, right to left: multiply in the product of everything AFTER i.
    running = 1;
    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        out[i] *= running;
        running *= a[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cout << out[i] << " \n"[i == n - 1];
}
```

In both passes, `out[i]` is assigned **before** `running` absorbs `a[i]` — that ordering is what excludes position `i` from its own answer. Swap the two lines and every entry includes itself.

## Dry run on `1 2 3 4`

After pass 1: `out = [1, 1, 2, 6]` — the running product of everything to the left.

After pass 2, walking backwards with `running` = 1, 4, 12, 24:

- `out[3] = 6 * 1 = 6`
- `out[2] = 2 * 4 = 8`
- `out[1] = 1 * 12 = 12`
- `out[0] = 1 * 24 = 24`

## Zeros fall out for free

No special case is needed. With one zero, every other position has it in either its prefix or its suffix, so they are 0; the zero's own position sees only the others. With two or more zeros, everything is 0. Verify against the test case `-1 1 0 -3 3`.

## The output idiom

`" \n"[i == n - 1]` indexes a two-character string: a space normally, a newline on the last element. It avoids a trailing space without an `if`.

