editorial:
## Step 1 is always the same

Count frequencies with a hash map. The interesting part is selecting the top `k` from those counts.

## Approach A: sort the counts — O(d log d)

Where `d` is the number of distinct values. Simple, and fast enough for almost every constraint.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;

    unordered_map<int, int> freq;
    freq.reserve(n * 2);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        ++freq[x];
    }

    vector<pair<int, int>> counts(freq.begin(), freq.end());   // {value, count}

    // 🛑 The comparator MUST break ties on the value, not just compare
    // frequencies. `counts` comes from an unordered_map, whose iteration order
    // is unspecified, so a frequency-only comparator makes the ANSWER depend on
    // the hash order — the same input could print `5 7` or `5 9`. Adding the
    // value as a tiebreak is what makes the result reproducible at all.
    nth_element(counts.begin(), counts.begin() + k - 1, counts.end(),
                [](const auto& x, const auto& y) {
                    if (x.second != y.second) return x.second > y.second;
                    return x.first < y.first;   // same frequency: smaller value wins
                });

    vector<int> top;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) top.push_back(counts[i].first);

    // The problem asks for ascending numeric order, so sort the values.
    sort(top.begin(), top.end());
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) cout << top[i] << " \n"[i == k - 1];
}
```

## Approach B: a min-heap of size k — O(d log k)

Better when `k` is much smaller than `d`, and the right choice on a stream where you cannot hold all counts.

The counter-intuitive part: to keep the `k` **largest**, use a **min**-heap. Its root is the weakest of the current best `k`, which is exactly the element to evict.

```cpp
priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> keep;  // {count, value}
for (auto [value, count] : freq) {
    keep.push({count, value});
    if ((int)keep.size() > k) keep.pop();      // drop the least frequent
}
```

## Approach C: bucket sort — O(n)

A count can be at most `n`, so index an array of buckets by frequency and walk it from the top. Linear time, and the asymptotically best answer.

```cpp
vector<vector<int>> bucket(n + 1);
for (auto [value, count] : freq) bucket[count].push_back(value);

vector<int> top;
for (int c = n; c >= 1 && (int)top.size() < k; --c)
    for (int value : bucket[c]) {
        top.push_back(value);
        if ((int)top.size() == k) break;
    }
```

## Which to use

| | Time | Space | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sort counts | O(d log d) | O(d) | Default; shortest to write |
| Min-heap of k | O(d log k) | O(k) | `k` much smaller than `d`, or streaming |
| Bucket sort | O(n) | O(n) | Asymptotically best; needs bounded counts |

## Pitfalls

- **`operator[]` on the frequency map inserts.** Here that is exactly what `++freq[x]` relies on, but reading a missing key elsewhere silently grows the map.
- **A max-heap for "top k largest".** You want a min-heap of size `k`.
- **Assuming `unordered_map` iteration order.** It is unspecified, which is why this problem mandates sorted output.

