## The easy O(n log n) answer

Sort, then scan counting consecutive neighbours while skipping duplicates. Perfectly acceptable, and what to write if the O(n) idea does not come to you.

## The O(n) idea

Put everything in a hash set. Then, for each value, only start counting if it is the **beginning** of a run — that is, if `x - 1` is absent.

That single condition is what makes the whole thing linear. Without it, the run `1 2 3 ... k` is walked from every one of its members, giving O(n²) on a single long run — a solution that looks linear and is not.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    unordered_set<long long> present;
    present.reserve(n * 2);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        present.insert(x);      // the set also deduplicates, for free
    }

    int best = 0;
    for (long long x : present) {
        // 🛑 Only run-starts do any walking. Every other value is skipped in
        // O(1), so the total work across all runs is O(n), not O(n^2).
        if (present.count(x - 1)) continue;

        long long cur = x;
        int length = 1;
        while (present.count(cur + 1)) {
            ++cur;
            ++length;
        }
        best = max(best, length);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
```

## Why the total is linear

Each value is visited by the outer loop once. The inner `while` only advances through members of the run whose start we are at, and each run is walked exactly once — from its smallest element. So across the whole execution the inner loop runs at most `n` times in total.

## Edge cases

- `n = 0` must print `0`. The loop body never executes, and `best` starts at 0.
- Duplicates: `1 1 2` has longest run 2, not 3. The set handles it without a special case.
- Negative values and values spanning zero: `-1 0 1` is a run of 3. Nothing special needed, which is why `long long` is used — `x - 1` at `-10^9` stays in range.

## Complexity

| | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Sort and scan | O(n log n) | O(1) extra |
| Hash set, run-starts only | O(n) average | O(n) |

The hash-set version is O(n) *average*. Against adversarial input designed to collide, it degrades — see the hash-table lesson. The sorting version has no such weakness, which is a real argument for it on a judge that allows hacks.
